::ALG1_U01_L01_GuidedPractice_Q01::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Evaluate 36<sup>1/2</sup>. {
=6 #Correct. The exponent 1/2 means square root, and sqrt(36)=6.
~18 #Not quite. This divides 36 by 2, but a 1/2 exponent means square root.
~72 #Not quite. This multiplies by 2, but the exponent 1/2 represents a root.
~1296 #Not quite. This squares 36, but 36<sup>1/2</sup> asks for the square root.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_GuidedPractice_Q02::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Evaluate 125<sup>1/3</sup>. {
~3 #Not quite. The denominator names the root; it is not the answer.
=5 #Correct. The exponent 1/3 means cube root, and cuberoot(125)=5.
~25 #Not quite. This divides instead of taking the cube root.
~375 #Not quite. This multiplies instead of applying the root.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_GuidedPractice_Q03::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Evaluate 64<sup>2/3</sup>. {
~8 #Not quite. This finds the cube root but does not apply the exponent 2.
~32 #Not quite. This treats the exponent like multiplication instead of root then power.
=16 #Correct. First take the cube root: cuberoot(64)=4. Then apply the power: 4<sup>2</sup>=16.
~4096 #Not quite. This squares 64 and ignores the cube root.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_GuidedPractice_Q04::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Which expression is equivalent to 9<sup>3/2</sup>? {
~sqrt(9<sup>2</sup>) #Not quite. This represents 9<sup>2/2</sup>, not 9<sup>3/2</sup>.
~9 + 3/2 #Not quite. A rational exponent does not mean addition.
~9<sup>3</sup> / 2 #Not quite. The denominator does not mean divide by 2.
=(sqrt(9))<sup>3</sup> #Correct. The denominator 2 gives the square root, and the numerator 3 gives the power.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_GuidedPractice_Q05::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Simplify 2<sup>1/3</sup> * 2<sup>2/3</sup>. {
=2 #Correct. Add exponents: 1/3 + 2/3 = 1, so 2<sup>1</sup> = 2.
~2<sup>2/9</sup> #Not quite. Exponents are added, not multiplied.
~4 #Not quite. This changes the base instead of applying exponent rules.
~2<sup>3/6</sup> #Not quite. 1/3 + 2/3 = 3/3, not 3/6.
}
